{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 23, "model_name": "meta/llama-4-maverick-17b-128e-instruct", "content": "the social network is a fascinating film that explores the origins of facebook and the complexities of its founder, mark zuckerberg. directed by david fincher, the movie is a gripping narrative that delves into the world of harvard university's elite and the cutthroat tech industry.\n\nthe film's strength lies in its nuanced portrayal of mark zuckerberg, played by jesse eisenberg. eisenberg brings a depth and complexity to the character, capturing his awkwardness, intelligence, and ambition. we see zuckerberg as a college student, struggling to navigate his relationships and his place in the world, and as a young entrepreneur, driven to succeed at all costs.\n\nthe supporting cast is equally impressive, with andrew garfield and justin timberlake delivering standout performances as eduardo saverin and sean parker, respectively. garfield brings a vulnerability and likability to saverin, making his eventual downfall all the more tragic. timberlake, on the other hand, is perfectly cast as the charismatic and confident parker, whose influence on zuckerberg is both inspiring and corrupting.\n\nthe film's script, written by aaron sorkin, is a masterclass in dialogue-driven storytelling. the conversations between characters are witty, insightful, and often uncomfortable, revealing the intricacies of their relationships and the tensions that drive the plot. sorkin's trademark rapid-fire dialogue is on full display, with characters trading barbs and witty one-liners that are both humorous and revealing.\n\nfincher's direction is, as always, meticulous and atmospheric. he brings a sense of tension and unease to the film, using a muted color palette and a pulsing electronic score to create a sense of unease. the cinematography is sleek and modern, capturing the sterile beauty of harvard's architecture and the frenetic energy of the tech world.\n\none of the most striking aspects of the film is its exploration of the themes of identity, class, and privilege. zuckerberg's journey is, in many ways, a classic rags-to-riches story, but one that is complicated by his elite upbringing and his complicated relationships with his peers. the film raises important questions about the nature of success, the costs of ambition, and the corrupting influence of power.\n\noverall, the social network is a thought-provoking and engaging film that is both a compelling drama and a nuanced exploration of the tech industry. with its talented cast, clever script, and meticulous direction, it's a must-see for anyone interested in the story of facebook and the complexities of its founder.", "logprobs": [{"token": "the", "logprob": -0.8832452893257141}, {"token": " social", "logprob": -0.0004891153075732291}, {"token": " network", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -0.006715467199683189}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -9.536738616588991e-07}, {"token": " fascinating", "logprob": -2.1516060829162598}, {"token": " film", "logprob": -0.018319223076105118}, {"token": " that", "logprob": -0.0005560758872888982}, {"token": " explores", "logprob": -0.7237313389778137}, {"token": " the", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " origins", "logprob": -0.6761552095413208}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.0004306104383431375}, {"token": " facebook", "logprob": -0.0005567907355725765}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -0.38687101006507874}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -2.753696753643453e-05}, {"token": " complexities", "logprob": -0.11532929539680481}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -4.768370445162873e-07}, {"token": " its", "logprob": -2.3841830625315197e-06}, {"token": " founder", "logprob": -0.2415265142917633}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.0003355178632773459}, {"token": " mark", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " zu", "logprob": -5.960446742392378e-06}, {"token": "cker", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "berg", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": ".", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": 